# Terms with a situational meaning in rocksdb-js. PR descriptions define them at first use as <ins title="…">term</ins>.
# One "term: definition" per line; keep each definition to one clause.
env: one Node.js environment, the main thread or a worker_thread, whose teardown must release its native state
DBDescriptor: process-global native state shared by every env that opens the same database path
DBHandle: one open's native handle onto a descriptor's column family, owned by a single env
tsfn: N-API thread-safe function that marshals a native completion back to its owning env's JS thread
column family: named keyspace of one database; db.use(name) returns a view, while transactions and backups span all
logical drop: column-family drop that is immediate for callers but physical only once no admitted commit names it
snapshot: read view a transaction pins, created on first read (optimistic) or first read or write (pessimistic)
verification table: process-global hashed slots of last-known record versions that let a cached read skip the get
write intent: per-key mark in the verification table while a transaction is mid-write; its release wakes parked retries
coordinated retry: a commit that loses a conflict parks until the holder's write intent releases, then reruns immediately
WriteBufferManager stall: writers parked on the manager's own queue, invisible to RocksDB's stall counters and writeStall event
user shared buffer: cross-thread ArrayBuffer keyed by name that lives until its column family closes, never by view lifetime
secondary: read-only follower of a live primary database, advanced by catchUpWithPrimary()
transaction log: append-only .txnlog files of caller-written entries, written just before a transaction's RocksDB commit
segment: one numbered .txnlog sequence file of a log store, rotated when it reaches its maximum size
txn.state: side file persisting the flushed position, whose sequence file anchors the retention floor
retention floor: txn.state's sequence file and every newer one; only files below it can be purged
committed position: position of the last transaction committed to RocksDB; reads of the log stop there
flushed position: log position up to which data has been flushed from memtables to RocksDB's on-disk files
torn tail: partial last write with nothing valid after it, truncated at open as the log's true end
mid-log break: corrupt frame with committed entries after it, reported as CorruptFrameError with a resync position
timestamp floor: clock raised at open above every batch key in the named originated log
monotonic timestamp: epoch-millisecond clock made strictly increasing; the transaction timestamp and log batch key
steady clock: elapsed-time clock, unrelated to wall time, for durations and deadlines instead of the monotonic timestamp
